    "result_text": "The full test suite passes \u2014 **281 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors, BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `namesakeCount` field, which reports how many other owners shared the owner's last name at the moment of registration.\n\nChanges made, following the existing pattern used for `membershipNumber`/`customerCode`/`membershipTier`:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the `namesakeCount` property (integer, `int32`, `minimum: 0`, `readOnly`) to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` gets a `setNamesakeCount`.\n\n2. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added a `namesakeCount(owner, allOwners)` static method that counts owners with a matching (case-insensitive) last name and a *smaller* id, plus a `@Mapping(target = \"namesakeCount\", ignore = true)` so MapStruct leaves it for explicit computation. Using `id < owner.id` excludes the owner itself and yields the count of *other* owners created before it. Since a new owner always has the largest id, this equals the namesake count at registration and stays stable when read back later (a later namesake has a larger id and is excluded).\n\n3. **Responders** \u2014 `RespondWithOwnerCreated`, `RespondWithOwner` (GET), and `RespondWithOwnerUpdated` (PUT) now call `setNamesakeCount(...)`. The GET responder is essential because the `Cp16Tests` create via POST and then read the value back via GET.\n\nThe `Cp16Tests` case now passes: the first owner with a fresh surname reports `namesakeCount = 0`, the second reports `1`, and no existing tests regressed.",
